{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"# HANDOFF — implemented, verified, NOT landed. Releasing the claim for a new session.\n\nOperator stop order: all work under epic `outrageous-uncultured-wildebeast` stops and hands off\nto a new session. Nothing was pushed or submitted. Transitioning this ticket back to **`open`**\nimmediately after this comment so the incoming session can claim it — a dead session's claim\nwould otherwise read as an abandoned ticket.\n\n## Where the work is\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| worktree | `/Users/joeoakhart/lamprey-libwrites` (branch `lamprey-libwrites`) |\n| commit | **`39c93ea3dc`** — \"Split _lib_writes by concern into three\" |\n| parent | `d2dea317ec` (current `origin/main` as of this handoff) |\n| tree | clean, nothing staged or stashed |\n\n**Correction to the sha in the stop order:** the commit is **`39c93ea3dc`**, not `1df39069fb`.\n`1df39069fb` was the pre-rebase sha from before the peak-window pause. On resume I rebased over\nthe six commits that landed during the window (`d2dea317ec`, `e0a55d5fac`, `846b7d860d`,\n`75d5edc39d`, `2b136e74e7`, `ec6ea55f0c`) — clean, no conflicts, and none of them touch\n`_lib_writes.py`, `__init__.py` or `_lib_gates.py`. Use `39c93ea3dc`.\n\n## What the commit contains\n\nThree-way split by concern, 800 -> **378 / 280 / 231**, all inside the 200-500 target band:\n\n| module | LOC | concerns |\n|---|---|---|\n| `_lib_writes.py` | 378 | genesis (`create_ticket`/`idea`) + status lifecycle (`transition`/`claim`/`reopen`) |\n| `_lib_mutations.py` | 280 | `_python_leaf` + its 7 callers, session logs, store maintenance |\n| `_lib_identity.py` | 231 | identity entities + key material, manifest signing |\n\nExactly three files changed. `__init__.py`, `_lib_gates.py` and everything under `tests/` are\nuntouched, because moved names are re-exported from `_lib_writes` with `# noqa: F401` (the\n`fsck.py` pattern).\n\n## Verification already done — do not redo\n\n- **Byte-identical proof.** Reconstructed the pre-split module's 31 top-level blocks from the\n  three new files and diffed the sets: every block identical, none added or lost. Only\n  docstrings, import headers and the re-export block are new text. This is stronger than \"the\n  suite passed\" and does not need repeating.\n- All **27** names resolve on **both** `rebar.<name>` and `rebar._lib_writes.<name>`, asserted\n  name by name including `_python_leaf`. Also confirmed `_lib_gates._python_leaf is\n  _lib_mutations._python_leaf` -> `True`, so the re-export chain is object-identical.\n- All **three** import orderings of the modules are clean (no cycle).\n- `make lint` and `make typecheck` green (mypy 527 files, no issues).\n- **Twelve** `scripts/check_*.py` gates pass. `check_build_env_locked` and\n  `check_commit_message_wrap` need CI-supplied args and were skipped, as in the fsck split; the\n  wrap rule was enforced anyway by the `commit-msg` hook, which rejected a 73-column first draft.\n- `check_complexity_baseline.py --lock` -> `OK (no raised ceilings or new entries)`; `--check` ->\n  `active=53 new=0 increased=0 stale=0`. **No re-key was needed** — the baseline holds zero\n  `_lib_writes.py` entries, verified *before* cutting, so the `--lock` hazard that cast\n  `Verified -1` on the fsck change cannot arise here.\n- All four `scripts/gen_*.py` generators re-run with no diff.\n- Commit hygiene: 38-char subject, DCO sign-off `RebarBotNava <joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com>`,\n  `Change-Id` stamped, `rebar verify-commit-ticket --rev HEAD` -> `OK - resolved\n  4631-5598-7127-4a56`.\n- All of the above was re-run **after** the rebase onto `d2dea317ec`, not just before it.\n\n## What remains\n\n1. **Re-run the CI selection from clean** — `pytest -m \"not integration and not external\" -n 3\n   --dist worksteal --timeout=300`. Honest state: **stopped mid-run for this handoff at 70%,\n   with zero FAILED and zero ERROR lines up to that point**. It is a partial pass, not a pass;\n   treat it as unrun.\n2. **`tests/integration`** — not started.\n3. **Rebase over current `origin/main`** (more will have landed) and re-check the three gates\n   that are base-sensitive: `--lock`, the generators, and `make typecheck`.\n4. **Push** `git push gerrit HEAD:refs/for/main`, land at `LLM-Review +1` **and** `Verified +1`,\n   submit via `POST /a/changes/<n>/submit` with the pinned `RebarBotNava` credential, realign the\n   worktree, tick the ACs, and close.\n\n## WARNING for the incoming session — the claim will NOT be a formality\n\nTwo distinct attestation hazards, in the order you will hit them:\n\n1. **`foreign_key` on this ticket's own plan-review attestation.** The certified PASS recorded\n   here was signed by environment `ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5`. A new session in a\n   different environment verifies it as **`foreign_key`**, not `certified`, so the claim gate\n   fails closed. **`sign-review` will not fix this** — it re-signs from the recorded sidecar but\n   cannot cross environments. The new session must run a **fresh `rebar review-plan\n   bloated-shadeful-lamprey`** (use `REBAR_LLM_BEDROCK_REGION=us-east-1`) to earn its own\n   attestation. The plan itself is settled and passed cleanly at 7,979 chars with **zero**\n   blocking findings, so expect a clean PASS with no plan edits.\n2. **The parent-first cascade.** Claiming this ticket cascades to epic `0e68-41eb-5782-4336`,\n   whose attestation has the same environment problem plus a currency one — see the HANDOFF\n   comment on the epic for the full recipe.\n\n## Session history worth carrying forward\n\n- The original blocker (epic description over the 8,000-char review admission cap added by\n  `7cf80009d9`) is **resolved**; `rollable-smoky-tsetsefly` covers it.\n- On resume the claim failed a *second* time on **base drift**, not the cap: the epic's\n  attestation went stale because `a147945aae` touched `docs/config.md`, which is in the epic's\n  dependency set. `rebar sign-review 0e68-41eb-5782-4336` fixed it with no LLM re-run. That\n  recipe works for benign code drift **within one environment** and is worth trying first — but\n  it will not help across environments (hazard 1 above)."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786645357548673001,"uuid":"1e617436-8539-48fc-8e6f-fd370b10b77f"}